<ProseLayout
  title="Editorial policy"
  metaTitle="Editorial policy — how formulas are chosen"
  description="How this site selects equations, what it cites, what it refuses to publish, and how often the content is reviewed."
  path="/editorial-policy"
  lastUpdated="2026-08-20"
  keywords={['editorial policy', 'how we source formulas']}
>
  <p>
    This site publishes calculators built on equations that were published, peer-reviewed where the
    field permits it, and are named on the page that uses them. This page sets out how those choices
    are made, and what will not be published here.
  </p>

  <h2>How equations are selected</h2>

  <p>
    Every formula on this site comes from a named, published source. Where a calculator offers more
    than one equation, all of them are shown rather than one being presented as the answer, because
    published equations for the same quantity routinely disagree — three basal metabolic rate
    equations differ by 100 to 300 kcal for the same person, and hiding that disagreement would
    misrepresent how much confidence the number deserves.
  </p>

  <p>
    The default equation on each page is chosen on published accuracy against a reference method,
    not on popularity. Where the most accurate equation needs an input most readers will not have —
    Katch-McArdle needs measured lean mass — the default is the best equation that works with the
    inputs people actually have, and the page says so.
  </p>

  <p>
    Every equation is listed with its citation in <a href="/">FORMULAS.md</a> in the repository, and each
    is checked against at least three worked examples from its source paper in the test suite. A change
    to a coefficient fails a test before it reaches a page.
  </p>

  <h2>What is cited, and what is not</h2>

  <p>
    Each calculator page names the paper, journal, volume and year for every equation it offers. If
    a value cannot be traced to a published source, it is not published as though it were.
  </p>

  <p>
    That principle has consequences worth stating plainly. The activity multipliers used by every
    TDEE calculator on the web — 1.2 through 1.9 — do not come from any single published paper. They
    are a convention that spread through fitness literature, and sites that attribute them to
    Harris-Benedict or Mifflin-St Jeor are citing papers that do not contain them. This site labels
    them as a convention and cites the physical activity level frameworks that <em>are</em>
    published, from the Institute of Medicine and FAO/WHO/UNU, alongside.
  </p>

  <h2>How currency is checked</h2>

  <p>
    Standards change, and a calculator that silently implements a rescinded standard is worse than
    no calculator. The Army body composition page is the clearest example: Army Directive 2026-13
    replaced the tape test with waist-to-height ratio in July 2026, and this site implements the
    current standard and explains the change rather than continuing to compute a figure the Army no
    longer accepts.
  </p>

  <p>
    Where a standard has been superseded, the superseded method is not implemented. Presenting a
    historical figure beside a current one invites exactly the confusion the page exists to resolve.
  </p>

  <h2>What this site does not publish</h2>

  <ul>
    <li>
      Guidance on very-low-calorie diets, prolonged fasting protocols, dehydration or water-cutting
      for weigh-ins, purging, or appetite suppression.
    </li>
    <li>
      Calorie targets below 1,500 kcal for men or 1,200 kcal for women. Intakes below those are
      clinical tools requiring supervision and monitoring that a website cannot provide.
    </li>
    <li>
      Plans toward a goal weight below a BMI of 18.5. The healthy range for that height is shown
      instead.
    </li>
    <li>
      Any calorie or body-composition figure for anyone under 18. The equations were derived on
      adults and do not model growth.
    </li>
    <li>
      Clinical tools whose outputs feed medical decisions: GFR, body surface area for chemotherapy
      dosing, and blood alcohol content. Those belong with a clinician, not a general-audience site.
    </li>
    <li>
      Pregnancy, due date, ovulation and conception calculators. Being wrong there matters more than
      almost anywhere else, and the category is properly served by established medical publishers.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>On authorship and review</h2>

  <p>
    There is no medical reviewer for this site, and no page claims one. You will not find "medically
    reviewed by" or an invented set of credentials anywhere here, because inventing them is
    dishonest and readers deserve to know what they are actually reading.
  </p>

  <p>
    The honest expertise signal available is the sourcing itself: every equation named, every
    citation checkable, every assumption and known limitation stated on the page that uses it. Where
    an equation is weak, the page says where and why — the ideal weight equations come from drug
    dosing and were never validated as health targets, and the page leads with that rather than
    burying it.
  </p>

  <h2>On the numbers themselves</h2>

  <p>
    Results are reported, not editorialised. This site tells you what an equation produced and what
    that equation assumes. It does not tell you whether the number is good, and it attaches no
    colour, icon or judgement to a result. BMI categories are stated once, factually, using the
    WHO's own terminology, alongside the fact that BMI cannot distinguish muscle from fat.
  </p>

  <h2>Corrections and review cadence</h2>

  <p>
    Every page carries a real last-reviewed date. Content is reviewed when a source standard
    changes, when an error is reported, and on a scheduled pass at least annually. Corrections are
    made to the page rather than appended, and the review date is updated when they are.
  </p>

  <p>
    If you have found an error — a mistyped coefficient, a misattributed citation, a superseded
    standard — <a href="/contact/">please report it</a>. Corrections to formulas are the most useful
    thing anyone can send.
  </p>
